course: dictionary
lesson: 2803
title: Disruption: when things stop going on
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Disruption is a noun. It means something that stops a thing from going on as it normally does.
explain: Read this: «In the UK a strike is legal after a vote, and the disruption is deliberate so that people notice.» The disruption is what stops things running as usual, such as trains or lessons. The sentence also tells you it is deliberate, which means it is done on purpose.
explain: Try the swap check. Put "something that stops things going on normally" in place of disruption. If the sentence still makes sense, your meaning fits.
fact: Disruption is a noun, and its plural is disruptions.
fact: «Disruption» means something that stops a thing from going on normally.
fact: A disruption is one such interruption, like a broken train that stops the day's travel.
fact: In school, a disruption is behaviour that stops the lesson carrying on as normal.
fact: «Deliberate» means done on purpose, so a deliberate disruption is one someone plans.
exercise: «Heavy snow caused disruption to the train times all morning.» What is the disruption? | The train being new and shiny | A ticket for the morning train | The station being painted | *The snow stopping the trains running as normal
exercise: Mrs Ahmed says the shouting from the back row was a disruption. What does she mean? | The children were learning a new song | The class finished early and went home | The lesson was calm and quiet | *The shouting stopped the lesson carrying on as normal
exercise: Which word means something that stops things going on normally? | Garden | Sunshine | Ticket | *Disruption
test: «The football match was called off after a power cut caused disruption to the lights.» What does disruption mean here? | A happy crowd cheering | A prize for the winning team | A bright light shining on the pitch | *Something that stopped the match going on normally
test: Which of these is a disruption to travel? | *A road closed by flooding, so buses cannot get through | A driver who smiles at everyone | A bus that runs on time | A bus stop painted blue
exam: «The disruption is deliberate, so that people notice.» What does deliberate mean? | Done very slowly | Done by accident | *Done on purpose | Done in the dark
